## Introduction
Structural color is a widespread phenomenon in natural creatures involving the relationship between structure and coloration, which depends on energy consumption and conversion between light and electrons, providing a principle for on-demand materials design [1,2]. Nanostructured photonic devices with brilliant structural colors have been developed in the way of photonic crystals (PCs), plasmonic nanoparticle (NP) arrays, Fabry-Pérot structures, etc. [3-5]. PCs are dielectric materials that exhibit a periodic refractive index (RI) variance and a distinctive photonic band gap, which serve as light waveguiding units for photonic communication and optoelectronic devices [6]. PCs  allow modulation of colors for sensor, display, anti-counterfeiting, and intelligent device applications [7,8]. One-dimensional (1D）PCs are comprised of two stacked materials with RI difference (Bragg bilayers) and exhibit optical band structures characterized by photonic bandgaps. Traditional 1D PCs comprised of inorganic materials or polymers are especially interesting for colorimetric sensing applications where changes in structural color can be induced by external stimuli of volatile organic compounds (VOCs) leading to changes in the RI or thickness changes of the layers [9,10]. Due to the insensitivity of stacking layers to the external environment stimuli, the 1D PCs sensor is difficult to perform real-time monitoring of the variation of the optical path length,which limits detection sensitivity for chemicals [11,12]. Consequently, an active component with a high specific surface area to construct the lD PCs is crucial for enhancing the detection sensitivity of chemicals.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are porous and crystalline structures consisting of organic ligands are arranged between metal-ion nodes [13,14]. MOFs have drawn great attention from the academy and industry in the past three decades because of their unique features, such as high surface area, steerable porous structure, and robust chemical tunability [15,l6]. Especially, MOFs are highly attractive for gas storage or separation [17], catalyzes [18]，photo-communication [19,20],optical filter [21, 22],and sensing [23,24]. Reports on PC sensors based on MOFs have been fabricated by integrating functional building blocks of MOFs and dielectric nanoparticles that can generate refractive index-dependent optical signals to analytes for sensing [25-27]. However, vacancies and structural defects form between the MOFs and the dielectric layers and/or inside the stacking layers own to the lattice constant mismatch of these different materials and their stacking method. MOF-on-MOF structure allows sequential growth of different types of MOFs with different lattice constants to sieve or filter molecules and reduce cross-talk in MOFbased sensorics [28-30]. However, the multilayered MOFs systems lead to increased incoherent interference with light and reduced tuning ability due to the RI indifference of MOFs and the elements such as dangling bonds at the interface acting as trap centers, complex centers and participating in nonradiative complexes [3l-33]. Thus, the challenge of MOFs-based PCs sensor lies in the strong incoherent scattering due to the mismatch oflattice constants between refractive layers [34-36].
Here we show the design of MOFs-based PCs with latticematched isostructural MOF thin films by a layer-by-layer assembly method, which demonstrates tunable structural colors, improved reflectivity, and enhanced detection sensitivity to chemicals. Ethanolic solutions of different types of NPs, such as $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}.$ $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2},$ and $\mathrm{{}}Z\mathrm{{nO}}$ were applied to modulate the RI of HKUST1 thin films (HKUST- $1@\mathrm{NPs}$ ）without changing the lattice parameters, porosities, and optical adsorption in visible light. The isostructural MOFs-based PCs were constructed by sequentially spraying coating low RI layer of HKUST-1 and high RI layer of HKUST- $1@\mathrm{NPs}$ to form the desired number of Bragg bilayers, which homogenously encapsulated NPs in certain domains of the multilayered HKUST-1 films. Bandwidth of the isostructural HKUST-1/HKUST-l@NPs PCs was tailored by the thickness and RI of HKUST-1 and HKUST- $\cdot1@\mathrm{NPs}$ layers,which demonstrate wide-range color tunable reflection. The isostructural MOFs-based PCs demonstrated excellent reflectivity of over $96\%$ . In addition, we achieved high detection sensitivity of chemicals on the isostructural MOFs-based PCs. Dynamic characteristics of sensing on the different types of MOFs-based PCs were demonstrated by characterizing the evolution of time-dependent reflectance. The results showed that the specific adsorption process of isostructural HKUST-1/HKUST-l@NPs PCs could be tailored for chemical sensing by hydrogen bonding interactions between -OH groups on the surface of NPs and chemicals.
