Numerical modeling using transfer matrix methods was employed to describe the influence of refractive index contrast on reflectance (Fig.1(c)). The simulated results (dash line) agree with the experimental results (solid line) of the HKUST-1/HKUST$\mathrm{1}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ PCs. Notably, the simulated results show multiple levels of interference peaks, while only one main peak appears in our experiment. The reason is that the finite difference time domain (FDTD) simulation cannot express the matching effect at the interface, which increases the chance of non-coherent interference [43]. So, the optical purity of the PCs is contributed to the design of the isostructural HKUST-1/ HKUST- $\mathrm{1}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ stacking without lattice mismatch between Bragg bilayers. Consistently, the optical photography showed variable coloration from blue to red on the PCs (Fig. 1(d)). Converting the reflection spectra of the HKUST1/HKUST- $\mathrm{1}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ PCs to CIE 193l chromaticity coordinates indicates good color tunability (Fig.l(e)). Thus, the reflection band of the isostructural MOFs-based PCs can be regulated via selective encapsulation of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs in spaced layer of HKUST-1 films to tune optical constants of the target domains over broad ranges.
Figure 2(a) shows the representative cross-sectional images of the isostructural HKUST-1/HKUST- $\mathrm{1}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ PCs with different number of Bragg bilayers. We observed monolithic films of the PCs with thickness of 190, 390, and $580\mathrm{nm}$ corresponding to the stacking number of bilayers from $N=1$ to 3. Notably, reflected color of the PCs with different Bragg bilayers $(N=1,2,$ and 3) was changing from blue-green to grass green and green, respectively (Fig. 2(a), inset). We further exploited reflection of the engineered isostructural HKUST-1/HKUST- $\boldsymbol{1}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ PCs at variable Bragg bilayers (Fig.2(b)). The reflectivity of one Bragg bilayer $(N=1)$ was about $57.54\%$ at $\lambda=505~\mathrm{{nm}}.$ ：which gradually increased to $96.48\%$ at $N=3$ . Also, full-width-at-half-maximum (FWHM) of reflection band of the PCs narrowed from 300 to $200~\mathrm{nm}$ In contrast, the reported MOFs-based PCs [44-47] were alternatingly stacked with nanoparticles of MOFs and the high RI materials with a clear multilayer interface by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) cross-section analysis, which led to more light scattering and decreased reflectivity of the PCs.
To confirm the multilayered structure of the isostructural HKUST-1/HKUST- $\mathrm{1}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ PCs, we conducted XPS depth profiling to resolve the material distribution profile perpendicular to the substrate (Figs. 2(c) and 2(d)). The energy-filtered transmission electron microscopy (EFTEM) technique is less quantitative for thin film analysis of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ in the high RI layer (HKUST- $\cdot1@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}.$ ）of the isostructural HKUST-1-based PCs due to their small concentrations across the domains. Instead, XPS depth profiling provides an alternative way to probe domain profiles in well-ordered nanostructures of the 1D PCs [48]. Figure $2(\mathrm{c})$ shows the alternating C 1s, Cu 2p, Si 2p, and $\mathrm{Ti}2\mathrm{p}$ signals of the isostructural MOFs-based PCs depending on the sputtering time. For the analysis, a diameter of $200~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ region was continuously analyzed using $\mathrm{Al}^{3+}$ ion source,while a concentric area of $600~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\times1200~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ was eroded with a $15~\mathrm{kV}$ Ar sputter beam up to a final pass energy of $112\ \mathrm{eV}$ The $\mathrm{Cu}\ 2\mathrm{P}$ signals characterized the metal-cluster of the HKUST-1 layers,which rose rapidly at the beginning of sputtering and decreased slowly to zero after a long period of stabilization. A significant decrease of the C ls signal was identified near the silicon substrate along with an increase of the $\mathrm{Si}~2\mathrm{p}$ signal from the Si substrate. Ti 2P signals characterized the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs of the HKUST- $\mathrm{1}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ films,which indicated the appearance of the high RI layers of the PCs. We zoomed in the alternating depth distribution of the Ti 2p spectra (Fig.2(d)). Notably, periodic distribution of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ signals fluctuation was clearly observed along with the sputtering time, in which the yellow shade represented the HKUST- $\mathrm{1}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layers and the blue shade corresponded to the HKUST-1 layers. Thus, XPS depth profiling proved periodic high and low RI layers of the isostructural HKUST-1/HKUST- $1@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\mathrm{PCs}$
Visual detection and sensitive discrimination of chemicals is a critical characteristic for the isostructural HKUST-1/HKUST$\mathrm{1}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ PCs. We introduced chemical vapor of analytes on the PCs to monitor the real-time change of optical reflection path by ultraviolet-visible spectrophotometry (UV-vis). Figure 3(a) shows the reflection spectra of the HKUST-1/HKUST- $\mathrm{1}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ PCs （ $70\mathrm{nm}$ HKUST-1/90 nm HKUST- $\mathrm{1}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2},$ ）with blue-green color during the ethanol (EtOH) sensing process. The primary reflection band (at $\lambda=465\mathrm{nm}$ ）experienced a red-shift to $535\mathrm{nm}$ within $5s$ when exposed to ethanol vapor, and the large wavelength shift $(70\mathrm{~\nm})$ demonstrated high sensitivity of the PCs. The corresponding color coordinates were presented from green to yellow,which was directly distinguishable by the naked eye (inset of Fig.3(a)). Also,we conducted acetone (ACE), hexane (Hex), isopropanol (IPA), trichloromethane (TCM),methanol (MeOH), dichloromethane (DCM), xylene (XYL), and toluene (TOL) sensing on the PCs, which showed a significant spectral red-shift of 16,17,37, 41,45,50,52, and $55\mathrm{nm}$ respectively (Fig. S6(a) in the ESM). The values of wavelength shift $(\Delta\lambda)$ and reflectance shift $(\Delta R)$ at the maximum reflectance were summarized to demonstrate a clear distinction for sensing these chemicals (Fig. S6(b) in the ESM). We performed principal component analysis (PCA) to the multivariate calibration of the characteristic chemical sensing of the PCs. A two-dimensional (2D） projection of the factor scores was calculated for each analyte (Fig.3(b)),whereas the principal component first (PCl) and the principal component second (PC2） account for the most significant variance of the measurements. Firstly, the parameters( $\Delta\lambda_{:}$ 、△R，△RI）were extracted from reflection spectra to analyze the individual capability of the PCs to discriminate between different analyte vapors (Table S2 in the ESM). Then, the PCl and PC2 were calculated to give the $x-$ and $y.$ -axis of the PCA plot (Table S3 in the ESM),which accounted for $93.2\%$ of the variance (Fig. 3(b)). We found a clear separation of all nine chemicals on the PCs, which indicated high sensitivity for discrimination of the analytes. Interestingly, sensing of the xylene had a strong dependence on PC2,while sensing of ethanol had great dependence on PC1. Report of HKUST-1 has specific adsorption for benzene molecules due to the host-guest interactions,while the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ encapsulated HKUST-1 film (HKUST- $\mathrm{1}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2})$ has selective adsorption for ethanol molecules due to hydrogen bonding interactions [39]. Based on the consistency of the above findings with our PCA patterns,we can equate PCl to the weighted effect of HKUST$\mathrm{1}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and PC2 to the weighted effect of HKUST-1 for chemical sensing. Thus, the isostructural HKUST-1/HKUST- $\mathrm{1}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ PCs discriminate between the investigated analytes and achieve specific adsorption on ethanol due to the existence of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs in HKUST-1. To investigate the stability and repeatability of chemical sensing on the HKUST-1/HKUST- $\mathrm{1}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ PCs，we tested the reversible reflection band shifts upon alternately exposing $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ and different chemicals for analysis (Fig.3(c) and Fig. S7 in the ESM). Quick response without showing significant variations of reflectance ( $\pm0.5\%$ in $\mathrm{R\%}$ ）wasobtained for 20 cycles repetition of the sensing process, suggesting that the resonators possessed excellent stability for sensing.
To evaluate the specific chemical sensing on the isostructural HKUST-1/HKUST- $\mathrm{1}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ PCs, we conducted dynamic sensing analysis by characterizing concentration dependent spectrum-shift of the different chemicals (Fig.3(d) and Figs. S8 and S9 in the ESM). The main reflection band of the HKUST-1/HKUST$\mathrm{1}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ PCs shifted from 465 to $536~\mathrm{nm}$ by gradually increasing ethanol concentration to $500\mathrm{ppm}$ which showed a linear increase with a slope of O.l5. We found that detection limit of the reflectance was $7.1\ \mathrm{ppm};$ .where the limit was defined as the corresponding concentration of analyte with a spectrum-shift of $1\ \mathrm{nm}$ [37]. We also calculated the detection limits for other chemicals $\mathrm{\TOL,}$ DCM, ACE, TCM, MeOH, XYL, IPA, and Hex) to be 9.1,10.0,16.7,8.3,11.1,13.5,20, and $25\mathrm{ppm}$ (Fig. 3(d) and Figs. S8 and S9 in the ESM). Figure 3(e) displays response time of ethanol sensing on the HKUST-1/HKUST- $\mathrm{1}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ PCs,which was calculated from the time-dependent reflectance recorded for every $0.5\mathrm{~s~}$ interval (Fig.3(e), insert). The reflection signals of the PCs kept flat in $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ flow,which tended to a marked decrease and then fluctuated around the same level in ethanol vapor flow. Response time and recovery time of the platform were defined as the differential absorption when the signal reached $95\%$ of the maximum absorption [49]. The response time of the PCs was 1.7, 3.9, 5.4, 7.4, and 9.8 s by exposure to $100{-}500\ \mathrm{ppm}$ ethanol vapors (Fig.Si0 in the ESM). Figure 3(f) shows the respective isotherms derived from the pressure dependent $\Delta\lambda$ responses of the HKUST-1/HKUST- $\mathrm{1}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ PCs to ethanol vapor of various concentrations. All of them display similarly the steep increase in ethanol uptake at a low relative pressure $(<~15~\mathrm{{\Pa})}$ and the saturation at $15\mathrm{-}25\mathrm{Pa}$ 。which indicated that ambient pressure has no effect on the adsorption process of the PCs. Thus, the isostructural HKUST-1/HKUST- $\mathrm{1}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ PCs  exhibit fast response time and litte response delay for chemical sensing, which exceeds the response of the reported MOFs-based sensor (Table S4 in the ESM).
