HKUST-1/HKUST- $\mathrm{1}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ PCs promote the highest sensitivity for the specific sensing of ethanol, which was consistent with the weighted effect of ${\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}{\mathrm{NPs}}$ on chemical sensing (Fig. 2(b)).
We further characterized the evolution of time-dependent maximum reflectance of the reflection band when ethanol vapors were exposed to the isostructural MOFs-based PCs with various different NPs encapsulations, independently(Fig. $5(\mathrm{b})_{.}^{.}$ ） Reflectance response of the PCs $(\Delta R_{\mathrm{max}}/R_{0}(\%))$ is defined as the ratio of maximum reflectance change $(\Delta R_{\mathrm{max}})$ to baseline resistance $(R_{0})$ . The response of HKUST-1/HKUST- $\mathrm{1}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ PCs varied from to $10.0\%-19.2\%$ upon exposure to 100, 200, 300, 400, and $500~\mathrm{ppm}$ of ethanol. The response of HKUST-1/HKUST$\mathrm{1}@\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}$ PCs and HKUST-1/HKUST- $1@Z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ PCs varied from $9.5\%$ to $12.4\%$ and $8.0\ \%$ to $11.3\ \%$ upon exposure to $100-$ $500\mathrm{ppm}$ of ethanol, respectively. The results indicate that HKUST1/HKUST- $\mathrm{1}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ PCs have a greater reflectance response to ethanol, implying a higher contribution of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs to ethanol sensing. Notably, the response of HKUST-1/HKUST- $\mathrm{1}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ PC increases linearly with increasing ethanol concentration, while the response of both HKUST-1/HKUST- $\mathrm{1}@\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}$ PCs and HKUST1/HKUST- $1@Z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ PCs firstly increases and then remains stable. Interestingly, the HKUST-1/HKUST- $\mathrm{1}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ PCs have the strongest response for specific ethanol sensing. From our previous reports, we proved that chemical sensing on the MOFs-based devices is affected by the following two factors: (i) replacement of gas molecules inside the MOFs cavity by the analyte; (ii) additional adsorption of the analyte to the encapsulated nanomaterials in the MOFs [39]. To elucidate the surface chemistry of the NPs on the specific chemical sensing of the HKUST-1/HKUST- $1@\mathrm{NPs}$ PCs, we conducted XPS analysis of the ${\mathrm{TiO}}_{2},$ $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2},$ and $\mathrm{{znO}}$ (Fig.5(c) and Fig.Sl6 in the ESM). O 1s region of the NPs can be fitted with three main peaks at binding energies of 530.8, 532.3,and $533.6~\mathrm{eV}_{:}$ which are attributed to the lattice oxygen in NPs $\mathrm{TiO}_{2},$ $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2},$ and $\mathrm{ZnO}\mathrm{\:}$ ,-OH groups, and C-O respectively [40-42]. XPS characterization of HKUST-1/HKUST- $1@\mathrm{NPs}$ PCs showed that the ratio of -OH/metal elements on the surface of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}(0.60)$ was greater than the $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}(0.50)$ and ZnO $\mathrm{NPs}(0.20)$ . The results suggest that the $-\mathrm{OH}$ groups on the surface of NPs can modify the adsorption of MOFs-based PCs to advance specific chemical sensing through hydrogen bonding interactions. Thus, we have expanded the versatility of MOFs-based PCs by constructing HKUST-1/HKUST-1 $@$ NPs PCs,which can be tailored for the sensing of chemicals.
## 3 Conclusion
In summary, we developed isostructural MOFs-based PCs by stacking Bragg bilayers of HKUST-1 and HKUST $\cdot1@\mathrm{NPs}$ thin films with the same MOFs lattice through a layer-by-layer assembly method. The monolithic HKUST-l was prepared as low RI layer by LPE method. High RI layer of the HKUST- $1@\mathrm{NPs}$ was achieved by sequentially spray-coating nanomaterials $(\mathrm{TiO}_{2},\mathrm{ZrO}_{2},\$ and $\mathrm{znO}$ NPs,independently） in HKUST-1 to form HKUST$\mathrm{1}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2},$ HKUST- ${\mathrm{1}}@{\mathrm{ZrO}}_{2},$ and HKUST- $\scriptstyle1@Z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ thin films, respectively. The isostructural MOFs-based PCs were constructure on a large scale by sequential spraying coating of the HKUST-1 and HKUST- $1@\mathrm{NPs}$ to form the desired number Bragg bilayers. Bandwidth of the isostructural HKUST-1/HKUST-l@NPs PCs were tailored by the thickness and RI of HKUST-l and HKUST$1@\mathrm{NPs}$ layers, which demonstrate wide-range color tunable reflection. Reflectivity of our isostructural MOFs-based PCs is about $96\%$ . In addition, we demonstrated high detection sensitivity for a variety of chemical sensing on the HKUST1/HKUST-l@NPs PCs. By characterizing the evolution of timedependent reflectance of the PCs, dynamic characteristics of chemical sensing showed that the specific adsorption process of the isostructural HKUST-1/HKUST-l@NPs PCs could be tailored due to the weighted effect of NPs for different chemicals. XPS measurement revealed that the specific sensing was induced by hydrogen bonding interactions between -OH groups on the surface of NPs and chemicals. We believe nanomaterials tailored isostructural MOFs-based PCs provide critical insights on totalreflectance integrated photonics, which is useful for real-time chemical sensing, security labeling, and smart display
